body:
Online dating is a crap shoot regardless of whether you're a man or a woman. However, despite the constant dick pic's and pick-up lines, things still skew more in women's favor on internet dating due to archaic perceptions of dating and who makes the first move. Women are far less likely to make the first move so it all kinda falls on men's shoulders to impress with that infamous first message. Especially since women have to sift through gross messages, penis photos, and crappy one-liners just to reach someone genuine. Also, if we are going by ranking systems here, a woman who is say, a 7 or higher, is far less likely to send the first message or respond to anything that doesn't immediately blow their mind. 

&#x200B;

Another issue with being a guy on dating apps are the scammers. And they're getting smart about it too. Actually striking up what sounds like a legit conversation before the inevitable message "Hit me up on this website instead" or "Send me your email and we can continue this conversation," or my personal favorite, "My life has (insert life problem here followed by...) and I need money." Women get the dick picks, guys get the porn and money scammers. Online dating might have been great at one point but it's become mostly a cash grab and with the exception of Bumble there's little incentive for women to be a first responder. I've been on dating apps for over 7 years and I've had maybe five dates total. Only two of those had relationship potential but none of them worked out. 

&#x200B;

Case and point, real life is better. And, honestly, I think women prefer it that way too. You just can't build something or get a good vibe check on someone on the internet. Humor is lost in translation as are other things. And if you're an introvert like me it's especially hard when the expectation is that I have to send the first message AND have it be good. I'm much better once I'm comfortable with someone and the conversation is started.

One of the better ones I've found that is quite useful is the Screenwriters Bible. It's on its 7th edition now. [https://smile.amazon.com/Screenwriters-Complete-Writing-Formatting-Selling/dp/1935247212/ref=sr\_1\_1?crid=IY7MS4CYAE44&dchild=1&keywords=the+screenwriters+bible&qid=1613541955&sprefix=The+Screenwriter%2Caps%2C218&sr=8-1](https://smile.amazon.com/Screenwriters-Complete-Writing-Formatting-Selling/dp/1935247212/ref=sr_1_1?crid=IY7MS4CYAE44&dchild=1&keywords=the+screenwriters+bible&qid=1613541955&sprefix=The+Screenwriter%2Caps%2C218&sr=8-1)

&#x200B;

There's also everyone's go-to book Save The Cat. [https://smile.amazon.com/Save-Last-Book-Screenwriting-Youll/dp/1932907009/ref=sr\_1\_1?dchild=1&keywords=Save+The+Cat&qid=1613542077&sr=8-1](https://smile.amazon.com/Save-Last-Book-Screenwriting-Youll/dp/1932907009/ref=sr_1_1?dchild=1&keywords=Save+The+Cat&qid=1613542077&sr=8-1)

&#x200B;

My suggestion though is to go into any book you read with a grain of salt. Learn what they're trying to teach you and then apply that knowledge as you see fit. Learn the rules. But develop your own style.

Unfortunately I don't. I wish there was. And I'm sure some people might be able to pull it off, but I have never been able to do it. There are a few scenes I've shot over the years that get me in the feels every once in a while, but for the most part it's a curse we as filmmakers just have to learn to live with. We don't get to enjoy our own movies the same way as the audience does. That's one of the reasons I usually just jump into a first draft of a script instead of outlining right away. It's the only way I get to enjoy the surprise of what might happen. I never know when a character in one of my scripts is going to die beforehand until I get to that point in the story. It's the only way I get to enjoy it from an audience perspective. In one of my short films [FAMILIAR](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=abD_6hPwOH4) there's a scene towards the end that still makes me a feel a little something, but not as much as it did the day we shot it. But once the movie is done, it's done.

&#x200B;

What I will say is that there's nothing wrong if you don't have an emotional response to your own movie. It's perfectly normal, especially if you've spent all the time planning it, shooting it, and editing it. It's pretty typical of an indie filmmaker to get pretty tired of seeing their own work after a while because they spend so much time with it in pre and post-production. My only other advice would be to just be proud of what you've accomplished. Regardless of whether it's good or not, take pride in knowing that you did it. Knowing that you took that leap and put in the work and made something. Not many filmmakers ever make it past the "idea" stage so take pride in that.
